+# Milestone #20 — Ball Integration Review
+
+- **Date:** 2026-08-10
+- **Model:** GPT-5.6 Sol, high reasoning
+- **Context:** Read-only review of the current `contraption-lab` working tree, focused on integrating Ball into the generic part/ownership model.
+
+## 1. Executive diagnosis
+
+Milestone #20 is only partially implemented.
+
+- All current built-in Balls are `"fixed"`, so non-selection and “Fixed parts…” feedback are correct for the current JSON: [prototype.json](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/src/levels/prototype.json:40), [medium.json](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/src/levels/medium.json:14), [hard.json](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/src/levels/hard.json:15).
+- The synthetic player-owned Ball E2E test bypasses the application reset flow. It calls `scene.resetLevel()` directly, leaving recreated parts input-disabled. This explains the test’s non-selection and non-dragging; it is not specifically a Ball pointer-routing failure.
+- The shared pointer/selection handler does support Ball when its registry entry is editable. Static tracing indicates a player-owned Ball loaded normally should select and drag.
+- There is a real #20 regression: Pause, Success, and Timeout can teleport the dynamic Ball back to its last Edit-layout position.
+- Initial load and normal UI Reset correctly enter Edit mode with the Edit button disabled. The button is an “enter Edit” command, so disabling it while already in Edit is semantically consistent.
+
+High confidence from source tracing. Browser tests were not executed because of the strict read-only requirement.
+
+## 2. Root causes
+
+### Selection and dragging
+
+The new E2E path mutates ownership and calls `scene.resetLevel()` directly: [puzzle.e2e.ts](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/e2e/puzzle.e2e.ts:504).
+
+`resetLevel()` recreates every part, but does not refresh interaction state: [PrototypeScene.resetLevel](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/src/game/PrototypeScene.ts:524). Each recreated shape is explicitly disabled by [prepareComponentInput](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/src/game/PrototypeScene.ts:1293). The normal UI Reset subsequently calls `applyState()`, which runs `setEditSelection()` and re-enables interactions: [main.ts](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/src/main.ts:133).
+
+Therefore the direct E2E reset leaves ramps, blocks, and Ball noninteractive. The test bypasses the behavior it intends to verify.
+
+When interaction is enabled, Ball is registered in the shared component map, its editability is derived from ownership, and it reaches the generic pointer handler: [createBall](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/src/game/PrototypeScene.ts:543), [handleComponentPointerDown](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/src/game/PrototypeScene.ts:778). Drag routing reaches `updateBallPosition()` correctly: [PrototypeScene.ts](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/src/game/PrototypeScene.ts:250).
+
+### Fixed feedback
+
+For current JSON, fixed feedback is expected.
+
+There is nevertheless a routing flaw: `if (!component?.editable)` treats both a genuinely fixed component and a missing registry entry as “fixed”: [PrototypeScene.ts](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/src/game/PrototypeScene.ts:787). Registry corruption or stale input could therefore produce a misleading fixed-part message for a player part.
+
+### Simulation-state regression
+
+`GameState.ballTransform` records the edited layout, not the live Matter position. Physics never updates it. Nevertheless, every `applyState()` calls `setBallTransform()`: [main.ts](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/src/main.ts:91).
+
+Pause, Success, and Timeout all invoke `applyState()`, so the live Ball is overwritten with its stale pre-run transform. This violates Pause semantics and can visually pull the Ball out of the goal after Success.
+
+Reset and Rerun restoration themselves are coherent in normal UI use: both scene and pure state restore their respective snapshots. The problem is unconditional synchronization during unrelated state changes.
+
+## 3. Current architecture problems
+
+- Ball participates in the generic pointer handler and selection display, but remains a separate singleton everywhere else.
+- `PlayerPartSnapshot` contains only Ramp and Block; Ball has its own parallel snapshot: [PrototypeScene.ts](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/src/game/PrototypeScene.ts:134).
+- State has separate `rampTransforms`, `blockTransforms`, and singleton `ballTransform`, with no Ball ID or ownership: [gameState.ts](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/src/state/gameState.ts:77).
+- Scene and pure state independently capture Run snapshots, creating two sources of truth.
+- Ramp/Block use `isEditablePart()`, while Ball duplicates the logic inline: [PrototypeScene.ts](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/src/game/PrototypeScene.ts:594).
+- Ball creation, spawning, removal, restoration, transform callbacks, inventory actions, and snapshots remain Ball-specific.
+- `spawnTrayBall()` hardcodes radius `24`, candidate positions, and a generated identity rather than deriving the spawned component from the JSON Ball definition.
+- The singleton `this.ball` prevents placing inventory while any Ball exists. Validation nevertheless accepts a placed Ball plus positive Ball inventory, producing an enabled tray count that cannot be used: [spawnTrayBall](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/src/game/PrototypeScene.ts:396), [validateLevel.ts](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/src/levels/validateLevel.ts:36).
+- Validation also permits incoherent combinations such as a fixed, initially-unplaced Ball or an initially-unplaced Ball with zero inventory.
+- Matter creation itself is sound: Ball remains a dynamic circle body, reset/rerun recreate it with fresh physics state, and contact registration follows the existing generic contact system.
+- The constant `BALL_LABEL = "prototype-ball"` remains a special assumption unrelated to the JSON Ball ID.
+
+The roadmap’s claim that #20 is completed and uses generic part state is therefore overstated: [ROADMAP.md](/home/anatolschwartz/code/contraption-lab/docs/ROADMAP.md:47).
+
+## 4. Recommended repair plan
+
+1. Replace the E2E setup with a genuine player-owned level fixture, or mutate the level and invoke the actual Reset button so `applyState()` runs. Do not call `scene.resetLevel()` as though it were the complete application Reset.
+2. Stop applying `ballTransform` during Running, Pause, Success, and Timeout. Synchronize it only during Edit-layout changes and explicit Reset/Rerun restoration. Define and test whether entering Edit restores the Run-start layout or adopts the live position.
+3. Use `isEditablePart()` for Ball and distinguish “registry entry missing” from “fixed part” in feedback routing.
+4. Centralize common part metadata—ID, kind, ownership, `fromTray`, shape, selection label—while retaining type-specific geometry and Matter creation.
+5. Make Ball identity/presence explicit in state and snapshots. Avoid relying on selection ID plus an unkeyed singleton transform.
+6. Define valid one-Ball inventory configurations, or move toward keyed Ball instances. Ensure tray UI cannot advertise unusable stock.
+7. Derive tray Ball geometry from the level/component definition rather than the hardcoded radius.
+8. Mark #20 complete in documentation only after the production player-owned path and simulation regressions are covered.
+
+## 5. Tests to add or change
+
+- Replace the private-scene mutation/reset E2E setup.
+- Player-owned preplaced Ball: initial selection, drag, selection feedback, overlap rejection, bounds clamping, removal, and inventory return.
+- Fixed Ball: click feedback, no selection, no drag, no removal.
+- Initially-unplaced Ball: tray placement, count decrement, removal increment, Reset, and Rerun with Ball present and absent.
+- Pause freezes the Ball at its live position without snapping.
+- Success and Timeout do not rewrite the live Ball transform.
+- Rerun restores Run-start position with fresh velocity; Reset restores JSON position, identity, ownership, and inventory.
+- UI Reset asserts `Mode: Edit`, Edit disabled, Run enabled, selection cleared, feedback cleared, and recreated player parts interactive.
+- Validator tests for invalid Ball ownership, Ball ID collision, negative/fractional Ball inventory, and incoherent placement/inventory combinations.
+- Matter integration test confirming fixed and player-owned Balls are both dynamic during Run.
+- Avoid using pure `gameState` tests as evidence for ownership enforcement: current state actions trust the caller and do not contain level ownership.
+
+## 6. Anything that should not be changed
+
+- Do not change built-in Ball ownership from fixed merely to make the test pass unless puzzle design explicitly changes.
+- Do not make a fixed-owned Ball a static Matter body; “fixed” means non-editable, not immobile during simulation.
+- Do not change gravity, collision parameters, goal detection, contact tags, known solutions, or add `matter-js`.
+- Do not perform a large general-purpose editor or multi-Ball refactor for this repair.
+- Do not enable the Edit button on initial load or after normal Reset while the application is already in Edit mode.
+
+The original review was conducted without modifying files. Its full prompt was not appended to `docs/CODEX_PROMPTS.md` because doing so would have violated the read-only instruction in effect at that time.
